WHY THIS MUTANT, AND WHY THIS ROW.

The anchor is #429's own comparison, `else if (idata > (uint64_t) TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY)`, at
src/devices/dev_rtc.c:131.  Flipping `>` to `<` leaves the file compiling and the clamp
present -- it clamps the WRONG SIDE, so every ordinary rate is forced to TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY
and every out-of-range one is narrowed exactly as the pre-#429 code did.

MEASURED, both arms, 2026-08-19.  *** THESE NUMBERS DESCRIBE THE 17-ROW TABLE.  They read
9 rows / 5 / 4 for one commit, against a table that had already grown to 12 -- a stale record
created by the very commit whose gate comment cites the sh4_tmu stale-floor incident. ***
  pristine                      17 rows, 0 failures, RTC_RANGE_PASS
  `>` flipped to `<`            17 rows, 11 failures, RTC_RANGE_FAIL
  the true pre-#429 revert      17 rows, 7 failures, RTC_RANGE_FAIL
    (the whole if/else-if/else replaced by the bare `d->hz = (int) idata;` that stood there
     before the round -- a stronger model of the defect than an `if (0)`, and it is the arm
     that proves the table sees the ORIGINAL bug rather than merely some edit.)

THE PIN IS "2^32: timer NOT removed" AND THE SUBSTRING MATTERS.  "timer NOT removed" alone
matches TWO rows (2^32 and 2^63) and would name a family rather than a row -- the exact
defect a reading seat found in two of the five original pins.  The 2^32 row is the one that
carries the defect's own mechanism: a guest write of 2^32 truncates to zero in the int, the
zero branch is taken, and an ADD silently becomes a REMOVE.

THE ONE-LINE FORM WAS CHOSEN OVER THE FULL REVERT because selfmutant.py requires a UNIQUE
anchor and both qualify, but the one-liner is the form every other fragment here uses and it
survives reindentation of the block around it.  The revert arm is not lost: it is recorded
above, and it is what the round's own measurement ran.
